You are debugging a production issue where an internal microservice experiences a slow, steady memory leak culminating in an `OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space`. The microservice implements a custom session tracking cache using `WeakHashMap` inside a Spring service:

java
@Service
public class UserSessionTracker {
    private final Map<UserSession, SessionContext> activeSessions = 
        Collections.synchronizedMap(new WeakHashMap<>());

    public void trackSession(UserSession session, String traceId) {
        activeSessions.put(session, new SessionContext(session, traceId, System.currentTimeMillis()));
    }

    public static class SessionContext {
        private final UserSession session;
        private final String traceId;
        private final long timestamp;

        public SessionContext(UserSession session, String traceId, long timestamp) {
            this.session = session;
            this.traceId = traceId;
            this.timestamp = timestamp;
        }
    }
}


Heap dump analysis reveals that even after sessions end and external references are discarded, millions of `UserSession` and `SessionContext` instances remain in memory.

You are debugging a production issue where an API gateway running on Java 17 experiences complete thread exhaustion and deadlocks under heavy traffic. Thread dumps show dozens of worker threads in the `WAITING` state on a `ConcurrentHashMap` internal lock. The component manages dynamic tenant configurations:

java
@Component
public class HierarchicalRoutingCache {
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, RouteConfig> routingMap = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public RouteConfig resolveRoute(String tenantId) {
        return routingMap.computeIfAbsent(tenantId, key -> {
            RouteConfig config = loadFromRemoteServer(key);
            if (config.hasParentFallback()) {
                // Recursively compute parent fallback configuration
                return routingMap.computeIfAbsent(config.getParentTenantId(), this::loadFromRemoteServer);
            }
            return config;
        });
    }

    private RouteConfig loadFromRemoteServer(String id) { /* network call */ return new RouteConfig(id); }
}


The deadlocks occur intermittently during cache misses, even when `tenantId` and `getParentTenantId()` are different strings.

You are debugging a production issue where an internal transactional auditing system fails to log entity state changes. Inside a Spring `@Transactional` service, entities are wrapped with custom monitoring proxies before being tracked in an identity-based collection:

java
@Service
public class OrderAuditService {
    private final Map<OrderEntity, AuditStatus> registry = new IdentityHashMap<>();

    @Transactional
    public void processOrders(List<OrderEntity> orders) {
        for (OrderEntity order : orders) {
            OrderEntity proxiedOrder = proxyFactory.createAuditingProxy(order);
            registry.put(proxiedOrder, AuditStatus.INITIALIZED);
        }

        // Downstream reconciliation phase
        for (OrderEntity order : orders) {
            AuditStatus status = registry.get(order);
            if (status == null) {
                auditFailureLogger.logMissing(order.getId());
            }
        }
    }
}


In production, `auditFailureLogger.logMissing()` is triggered for every single order, despite all orders being processed and having identical `equals()` and `hashCode()` values before and after proxying.
